      "latency_ms": 17512.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 28,
      "completion_tokens": 1115,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Molecular Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule is **bent (V-shaped)** at an angle of ~104.5°. Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is **polar**—it has a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity allows water molecules to stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion) via **hydrogen bonds**. This relatively weak but abundant bonding is responsible for almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Physical States (Phases)\nWater is one of the few substances that exists naturally on Earth in all three states:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Freezes at **0°C (32°F)** at standard pressure. Unusually, ice is **less dense** than liquid water (it expands ~9% upon freezing), causing it to float. This insulates bodies of water, allowing life to survive underneath.\n*   **Liquid (Water):** Exists between 0°C and 100°C. It has a very high **surface tension** and **specific heat capacity**.\n*   **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam):** Boils at **100°C (212°F)** at standard pressure. It is a potent greenhouse gas.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Key Anomalous Properties (Why Water is \"Weird\")\nWater behaves differently than almost any other liquid due to hydrogen bonding:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Its polarity allows it to dissolve more substances than any other liquid (salts, sugars, acids, bases, gases), facilitating chemical reactions and nutrient transport.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** It takes a lot of energy to heat water up. This stabilizes Earth’s climate (oceans absorb heat by day/release by night) and regulates organism body temperatures.\n*   **High Heat of Vaporization:** Evaporating water absorbs massive heat (evaporative cooling/sweating).\n*   **Density Maximum at 4°C:** Liquid water is densest at 4°C. As it cools toward freezing, it expands. This causes **lake turnover** and prevents lakes from freezing solid from the bottom up.\n*   **Cohesion & Adhesion:** Allows **capillary action** (water moving up plant roots/stems against gravity).\n\n---\n\n### 4. Biological Role\n*   **Medium for Metabolism:** Almost all biochemical reactions (hydrolysis, condensation, photosynthesis, respiration) occur in aqueous solution.\n*   **Transport:** Blood (mostly water) transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, and waste.\n*   **Structural Support:** Turgor pressure in plant cells and hydrostatic skeletons in worms/jellyfish rely on water incompressibility.\n*   **Temperature Regulation:** Sweating, panting, and transpiration cool organisms.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Planetary Distribution (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **~71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water.\n*   **97.5% is Saline** (oceans).\n*   **2.5% is Freshwater**, of which:\n    *   ~68.7% is locked in ice caps/glaciers.\n    *   ~30.1% is groundwater.\n    *   ~0.3% is surface water (lakes, rivers, swamps) + atmospheric water.\n*   **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Continuous movement via evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, and runoff.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Human Use & Significance\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawals (irrigation).\n*   **Industry:** ~19% (cooling, processing, manufacturing).\n*   **Domestic:** ~11% (drinking, sanitation, hygiene).\n*   **Energy:** Hydroelectric power; cooling for thermal/nuclear plants.\n*   **Health:** Access to clean water and sanitation (SDG 6) is a fundamental human right and primary driver of public health.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Definition\n> **Water (H₂O) is a polar inorganic compound that is a liquid at standard temperature and pressure. Its unique hydrogen-bonded structure gives it anomalous properties—high heat capacity, universal solvent capability, and a solid phase less dense than its liquid phase—that make it the indispensable solvent and structural medium for terrestrial life and the primary regulator of Earth's climate.**"
